// LinuxRuntime — the Linux facts the host surface reads that are the SAME on x86-64 and on AArch64: the
// `*at` anchor, the three `open` flag words the generic header defines, the layout of the search object the
// backends invent for themselves, and `struct linux_dirent64`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A CONSTANT BELONGS HERE ONLY IF THE TWO ARCHES WERE MEASURED TO AGREE, AND MOST DO NOT.** Every
// syscall NUMBER differs (`getpriority`/`setpriority` are even 140/141 one way and 141/140 the other, so the
// same number names the opposite call); `O_DIRECTORY` is `0o200000` on x86-64 and `0o40000` on AArch64,
// which `arch/arm64/include/uapi/asm/fcntl.h` overrides for AArch32 compat; and `struct stat` is 144 bytes
// with `st_mode` at 24 on x86-64 against 128 bytes with `st_mode` at 16 on AArch64 — while `st_size` and the
// three timestamps land at the same offsets on both, which is exactly the partial agreement that would let a
// wrongly-shared layout pass every test until `File.info()` read a mode out of `st_uid`. Those stay with
// their arch, under an `X64Linux`/`Arm64Linux` prefix, because a number that differs must not be reachable
// under a name that does not say so.
//
// ⭐ **THE POSIX HALF IS `Targets/Shared/PosixRuntime.maxon`** — the errnos, the `st_mode` bits, the glob
// bytes and `PosixStatLayout` are true of Darwin as well and are not Linux's to state.

// `AT_FDCWD`, as the two's-complement word a `mov` materialises: every `*at` call on either lane anchors
// here, which is what makes it the plain call it replaces. It is `linux/fcntl.h`'s, not an arch header's, and
// each lane's `openat` was invoked BY NUMBER through it and answered a live descriptor for a real path.
// (`MachineWord` is unsigned — `MmapAnonFd` is the same spelling one file over, for the same reason.)
export let LinuxAtFdCwd = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFF9C

// --- `open` flags, from `asm-generic/fcntl.h` ---
//
// ⚠ **THESE ARE NOT DARWIN'S BIT VALUES AND THE DIFFERENCE IS SILENT.** `O_CREAT` is 0x40 here and 0x200
// there, `O_TRUNC` 0x200 here and 0x400 there — v1 carries a whole branchless translator for exactly this
// (`emitMacosOpenFlagXlateArm64`) and records what a wrong bit does: a write open lands `O_ASYNC` instead of
// `O_TRUNC`. shv2 needs no translator because the macOS lane calls `creat` rather than `open` and never
// spells a flag word at all; these are Linux's alone.
//
// ⚠ **AND THEY ARE THE ARCH-NEUTRAL THREE.** AArch64's `asm/fcntl.h` overrides exactly four flags before
// including the generic header — `O_DIRECTORY`, `O_NOFOLLOW`, `O_DIRECT`, `O_LARGEFILE` — and none of them
// appears in either word below, so both arches read the generic values. `O_DIRECTORY` is per-arch for that
// reason and is declared beside its arch's syscall table.
//
// `O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC` IS `CreateFileA(GENERIC_WRITE, CREATE_ALWAYS)`, which is the same trio `creat`
// abbreviates on the Darwin lane — so the two opens agree on semantics while spelling them differently.
//
// ⚠ **THE READ-ONLY FLAG WORD IS NOT HERE.** `O_RDONLY` is 0 on Darwin as well, so it is `PosixRuntime`'s
// `PosixOpenReadOnly` and not Linux's to state a second time.
export let LinuxOpenCreateTruncateFlags = 0x241

// `unlinkat`'s flag word: 0 means "remove a FILE". (`AT_REMOVEDIR` is what would make it an `rmdir`, and
// this op is `DeleteFileA`, which refuses a directory.)
export let LinuxUnlinkFileFlags = 0

// `ENAMETOOLONG` — 36 on Linux and 63 on Darwin, so unlike the five in `PosixRuntime` it cannot be a POSIX
// fact; it is the same 36 on both Linux arches because neither overrides `asm-generic/errno.h` (each arch's
// `asm/errno.h` is a bare include of it). It is what a search whose DIRECTORY PART does not fit the scratch
// reports; see either lane's `…DirPrefixChunk` for why that is a refusal rather than a truncation.
export let LinuxErrnoNameTooLong = 36

// ⭐ **THE `.data` WORD EACH LANE'S HOST SURFACE STASHES A FAILED SYSCALL'S REASON INTO.**
// `StdOp.osLastError` is a SEPARATE op emitted immediately after the call that failed, so the reason must
// survive from one to the other; a syscall reports it in the return register and keeps no copy, and a raw
// static image links no libc to hold one. The LABEL is one name because exactly one lane's runtime reaches
// any image; what sits behind it is the lane's own choice — a process-wide word where there is one thread,
// a per-OS-thread scheduler slot where a real `clone` can make more.
export let LinuxErrnoLabel = b"__mrt_errno"

// ⭐ **WHO READS THE errno WORD — the union of the facilities that emit `StdOp.osLastError`, and therefore
// the gate on `mrt_host_last_error` itself.** `usesIocp` is among them: `__gt_subp_yield_read` classifies a
// failed pipe read with it, and a spawn failure is reported through it.
export function linuxUsesHostSurface(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return usage.usesManagedFile or usage.usesManagedDirectory or usage.usesConsoleStdin or usage.usesIocp
end 'linuxUsesHostSurface'

// ⭐⭐ **WHO TOUCHES IT AT ALL — the readers above, PLUS the bodies that only ever STASH.** DERIVED from its
// own subset rather than restated, so the `.data` layout cannot come apart from the chunk list.
//
// ⛔ **THE STASH-ONLY PRODUCER IS THE SPAWN, AND IT RIDES `usesGt`.** `installGtRuntime` builds
// `__gt_process_run` unconditionally, so `mrt_host_spawn` — whose failure arm stashes an errno — is laid out
// in every green-thread program, including one that reads no pipe and opens no file. MEASURED: a
// `runProcess("exit 3")` that discards its child's output sets `usesGt` and NOTHING else, and gating the word
// on the reader union alone answered `GlobalDataTable.dataSectionOffsetOf: no .data slot is labelled
// '__mrt_errno'` at BACKEND time — a panic and not a diagnostic.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH LINUX LANES ASK THESE TWO, AND NEITHER OWNS THEM.** The question is about `RuntimeUsage` and
// names no instruction set; a second copy beside one assembler is a facility added to one lane's gate and
// not the other's, which is that lane's `.data` word missing under a chunk that stashes into it.
// ⚠ **THE SOCKET BAND IS A SECOND STASH-ONLY PRODUCER**, and for the reason `linuxOpensFiles` states: its
// resolver opens `/etc/hosts` through `mrt_host_file_open`, whose failure arm stashes an errno nothing in a
// socket program will ever read back.
export function linuxTouchesErrnoWord(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return linuxUsesHostSurface(usage) or usage.usesGt or usage.usesManagedSocket
end 'linuxTouchesErrnoWord'

// ⭐⭐ **WHO REACHES `mrt_host_file_open`, AND THE THIRD PRODUCER IS NOT A `File` CALL.** `usesIocp` is one
// (a subprocess stream redirected to a path is opened here), and on this lane the SOCKET band is another:
// with no `getaddrinfo` to call, `ManagedSocketRuntime`'s built-in resolver reads `/etc/hosts` and
// `/etc/resolv.conf` through the ordinary file ops — so a program that opens no `File` and spawns nothing
// still branches into this chunk. Gating it on `usesManagedFile` alone is a `bl` to a symbol nothing laid
// out, which `arm64ResolveCallFixups` reports as a PANIC rather than a diagnostic.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH LINUX LANES ASK IT AND NEITHER OWNS IT**, exactly as `linuxTouchesErrnoWord` records for the
// errno word: the question is about `RuntimeUsage` and names no instruction set, and a second copy beside
// one assembler is a producer added to one lane's gate and not the other's.
export function linuxOpensFiles(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return usage.usesManagedFile or usage.usesIocp or usage.usesManagedSocket
end 'linuxOpensFiles'

// The read chunk's gate — every opener above, plus standard input, which is a descriptor this surface reads
// without ever opening one. DERIVED from its own subset so the two cannot come apart.
export function linuxReadsFiles(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return linuxOpensFiles(usage) or usage.usesConsoleStdin
end 'linuxReadsFiles'

// ⭐⭐ **THE `.data` WORD HOLDING THE ENVIRONMENT VECTOR, AND ONLY A LIBC-LESS LANE NEEDS ONE.** Windows asks
// `GetEnvironmentVariableA` and macOS asks `getenv`; a static Linux image has neither, so the kernel's
// `envp` is reachable exactly once — in the entry stub, where it still sits on the stack — and
// `mrt_host_env_read` reads it back out of this word. Both Linux lanes capture into the same name because
// only one lane's runtime ever reaches an image.
export let LinuxEnvpLabel = b"__mrt_envp"

// `'='` — what separates a name from its value in every `envp` entry. Both lanes' walks compare against it,
// and a match is the name followed by THIS byte: comparing only the prefix makes `MAXON_MAX` match
// `MAXON_MAX_PROCS`.
export let LinuxEnvSeparatorByte = 0x3D

// --- The initial process stack `execve` hands the entry stub ---
//
// `[sp]` holds argc, `sp + 8` IS the argument vector, its NUL terminator follows, and the environment
// begins after that — so `envp` is at `sp + (argc + 2) * 8`. The layout is the System V ELF ABI's and no
// architecture varies it, which is why both entry stubs read these rather than each spelling them.
export let LinuxEntryArgvOffset = SpillSlotBytes as Imm32
export let LinuxEntryEnvpSkippedWords = 2 as Imm32

// `sizeof(sigset_t)` — 64 signals, one bit each, on every Linux architecture. `rt_sigaction` takes it as
// its fourth argument and `ppoll` as its last, and the kernel checks it even when the mask is NULL.
export let LinuxSigsetBytes = 8

// SIGFPE — the signal an integer divide fault is delivered as, and 8 on every Linux architecture. Here rather
// than in a backend for `LinuxSigsetBytes`'s reason: there is one fact, so there is one name for it.
export let LinuxSignalFpe = 8

// `struct timespec` — `tv_sec` then `tv_nsec`, two 64-bit words, which is two of either assembler's 8-byte
// frame slots. Each clock chunk holds its out-param pointer in slot 0 and reserves these above it. HOW the
// two fields are then addressed is per-arch: the frames grow in opposite directions, so each lane derives
// its own offsets (see either `…ReadClockTimespec`).
export let LinuxTimespecSlots = 2 as StackSize

// The `.data` word above, laid out for the programs that read the environment. Gated on the FACILITIES that
// reach it rather than on the target alone, exactly as each lane's errno word is: a Linux program that
// neither schedules nor asks about the terminal never touches it, so it does not carry it.
//
// ⚠ **NO `os` TEST INSIDE THE `usage` TEST — the target is asked FIRST**, because the answer really does
// depend on both and this is the one place that pair is stated (`commandLineRuntimeGlobals`' arrangement).
export function linuxEnvpGlobals(usage RuntimeUsage, target Target) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	if target.os != Os.linux or not posixReadsEnvironment(usage) 'notThisLane'
		return out
	end 'notThisLane'

	// Written ONCE, in `mrt_start`, before `main` and therefore before anything could make a second OS
	// thread; every later access is a read.
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: LinuxEnvpLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	return out
end 'linuxEnvpGlobals'

// ==========================================================================
// Is this descriptor a terminal?
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`isatty` IS `ioctl(fd, TCGETS, &termios)`, AND A LIBC-LESS IMAGE MAKES THAT CALL ITSELF.** The
// request word is `0x5401` on both Linux arches (neither overrides `asm-generic/ioctls.h`) and the kernel
// writes **36 bytes** — `struct termios` — through the pointer, so the body needs a frame rather than two
// inline instructions. Only the syscall NUMBER differs, and it stays with its arch.
//
// ⚠ MEASURED by raw syscall on both guests against a 0xAA-filled buffer: `0` on a pty slave and a pty
// master, `-ENOTTY` on a pipe, a regular file and `/dev/null`, agreeing with `isatty` on all five, and 36
// bytes overwritten.
//
// ⛔ **`0x5402` IS `TCSETS`, WHICH ALSO ANSWERS `0` ON A TERMINAL — AND WRITES THE BUFFER INTO ITS
// SETTINGS.** An off-by-one here corrupts the user's terminal instead of answering the wrong question,
// which is why the request is named rather than spelled at its two call sites.
export let LinuxIoctlTcGets = 0x5401
export let LinuxTermiosBytes = 36

// The two chunks no Darwin lane spells, so they keep a lane infix rather than the shared `mrt_host_` prefix
// (`PosixRuntime`'s header states that rule): one exists because a static image has no `fnmatch` and the
// other because it has no `malloc`.
export let LinuxGlobMatchName = b"mrt_linux_glob_match"
export let LinuxDirPrefixName = b"mrt_linux_dir_prefix"

// ==========================================================================
// The clocks, the running image and the machine query
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE `clockid_t` VALUES, AND THEY ARE LINUX'S RATHER THAN EITHER ARCH'S.** No architecture
// overrides `linux/time.h`, so one set serves both lanes — unlike the syscall NUMBERS that carry them, which
// differ (`clock_gettime` is 113 on AArch64 and 228 on x86-64) and stay with their arch.
//
// ⛔ **A WRONG ID DOES NOT FAIL. It reads a DIFFERENT CLOCK and answers a plausible wrong time**, which is
// why each is named rather than spelled at its call site. `CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID` is 3 here and 16 on
// Darwin, and the two Darwin/Linux monotonic clocks INVERT their names: Linux's `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` does not
// advance across a suspend and `CLOCK_BOOTTIME` does, so the id matching Darwin's `CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW`
// SEMANTICS is this one — taking `CLOCK_BOOTTIME` "because Darwin takes the raw clock" would be reading the
// name instead of the property.
//
// ⚠ MEASURED by number on both guests: id 0 answered a real calendar time, id 1 a positive uptime, id 3 a
// few tens of milliseconds in a freshly started process.
export let LinuxClockRealtime = 0
export let LinuxClockMonotonic = 1
export let LinuxClockThreadCpuTime = 3

// The symlink whose target IS the running image's path — the whole of `osExecutablePath` on Linux, because
// `_NSGetExecutablePath` has no counterpart here and no call answers the question.
export let LinuxProcSelfExe = b"/proc/self/exe"

// ⭐⭐ **`cpu_set_t` IS 128 BYTES, AND `sched_getaffinity` REFUSES A SMALLER ONE.** The kernel demands a size
// that is a multiple of a word AND large enough for every possible CPU, answering `-EINVAL` otherwise; 128
// bytes is glibc's `cpu_set_t` and covers 1024 CPUs, which is the size every caller passes. It answers the
// number of BYTES it filled, not a count of CPUs — the count is a POPCOUNT over those bytes.
//
// ⚠ The buffer is zeroed before the call so the popcount may sweep the WHOLE 128 bytes rather than only the
// prefix the kernel wrote. Sweeping a fixed extent is one loop with one bound; sweeping the returned prefix
// is the same loop plus a rounding of a byte count up to a word, and the two can disagree.
export let LinuxCpuSetBytes = 128

// What a failed `sched_getaffinity` answers, in the vocabulary the Std tier ALREADY reads.
// `CpuParallelRuntime.buildCpuCount` clamps `< 1` to 1 with a SIGNED test written to cover both Windows's 0
// and `sysconf`'s -1, so each Linux lane reports its own failure as that same -1 rather than inventing a
// floor here — which would be one contract in two places (`lowerOsCpuCount` states the rule).
export let LinuxCpuCountUnavailable = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

// `sched_getaffinity`'s `pid` argument. 0 means the CALLING thread, which is the only process either chunk
// may ask about — and is why the syscall needs no `getpid` ahead of it.
export let LinuxAffinityWhoIsSelf = 0

// ⭐⭐ **`getpriority`'s SYSCALL DOES NOT ANSWER A NICE VALUE — IT ANSWERS `20 - nice`.** A syscall reports
// failure as a small negative number, and a nice value is legitimately negative, so the kernel biases the
// answer into a non-negative range and every libc subtracts the bias back off. Each lane's
// `lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriorityLinux` is that libc. ⚠ The macOS lane needs no such fold because it calls
// the LIBRARY function, which has already done it — this is a raw-syscall fact, which is why it is here and
// not beside `PosixBackgroundNiceValue`.
//
// ⚠ **IT WOULD HAVE HIDDEN ITSELF AT EXACTLY THAT NICE VALUE:** `20 - 10` is 10, so a lowering that forgot
// the fold still answers 10 for the one value the specs pin, and is wrong for every other. MEASURED on both
// guests: a process at nice 7 read back 13.
export let LinuxGetPriorityBias = 20

// ==========================================================================
// The search object
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **ONE `mmap`'d PAGE HOLDS THE WHOLE SEARCH, AND THAT IS THE ANSWER TO HAVING NO `malloc`.** Win32's
// `HANDLE` carries the open directory and the pattern still to be matched; this page carries all four things
// a walk needs — the descriptor, the cursor and filled count into the `getdents64` buffer, the pattern, and
// the directory-prefix scratch — and one `munmap` releases all four.
//
// ⭐ **THE GEOMETRY IS THE BACKENDS' OWN INVENTION, NOT THE KERNEL'S**, which is why it is arch-neutral by
// construction rather than by measurement: nothing outside these chunks ever reads this page.
//
// ⚠ **A NEGATIVE `dirfd` IS THE EXHAUSTED SINGLE-`stat` SEARCH, NOT AN ERROR.** A pattern with no wildcard is
// answered by one `stat` and has exactly one entry, which `FindFirstFileA` has already reported — so the
// object it hands back has no directory to walk and the first `FindNextFileA` must report end-of-iteration
// rather than call `getdents64(-1)`. Darwin spells the same fact as a NULL `DIR*`; here 0 is a legal
// descriptor, so the sentinel is -1.
//
// ⚠ **`MAP_ANON` PAGES ARE KERNEL-ZEROED**, which is why only `dirfd` is written at birth: the cursor, the
// filled count and the pattern's first byte are already 0 by the mapping's own contract (the one
// `PosixMmap`'s header states for the slab).
export let LinuxSearchDirFdOffset = 0x00 as Imm32
export let LinuxSearchCursorOffset = 0x08 as Imm32
export let LinuxSearchFilledOffset = 0x10 as Imm32
export let LinuxSearchPatternOffset = 0x18 as Imm32
export let LinuxSearchPrefixOffset = 0x200 as Imm32
export let LinuxSearchEntriesOffset = 0x400 as Imm32
export let LinuxSearchBytes = 0x1000

// What the `getdents64` buffer may hold: the whole tail of the page above the two variable-length regions.
export let LinuxSearchEntriesBytes = LinuxSearchBytes - (LinuxSearchEntriesOffset as ParsedInt)

// The `dirfd` of a search that has no directory to walk. DERIVED from the handle sentinel rather than
// re-spelled, because both mean "there is nothing here" and the tests that read them are in different files:
// this one is a signed `< 0` inside the chunks, `InvalidHandleValue` is an `== -1` in the Std tier.
export let LinuxNoDirectory = PosixInvalidHandle

// `struct linux_dirent64` — `d_ino@0`, `d_off@8`, `d_reclen@16` (u16), `d_type@18` (u8), `d_name@19`. The
// kernel defines it once in `include/linux/dirent.h` for every architecture, and it was READ BACK on each
// lane: the first records of a real directory decoded to `.`, `..` and real file names at exactly these
// offsets.
//
// ⚠ **`d_reclen` IS READ AS THE 32-BIT WORD AT 16 AND MASKED** — the other 16 bits of that word are `d_type`
// and the name's first byte, which the mask discards. (The AArch64 assembler has `ldr Wt` and `ldrb Wt` and
// no halfword form, and x86-64 takes the same route so the two walks stay one algorithm.)
//
// ⚠ Only the NAME is read: `d_type` would answer "is it a directory?" one call cheaper than `fstatat`, and
// the record needs a SIZE and three TIMESTAMPS from that call regardless, so a `d_type` fast path would be a
// second source for one of six fields — `Arm64DarwinRuntime` makes the same argument about `readdir`'s.
export let LinuxDirentRecordLengthOffset = 16 as Imm32
export let LinuxDirentNameOffset = 19 as Imm32
export let LinuxDirentRecordLengthMask = 0xFFFF as Imm32

// ⭐ **THE SEARCH PAGE'S THREE REGIONS MUST EACH HOLD WHAT IS WRITTEN INTO THEM, AND NOTHING ELSE CHECKS.**
// The pattern copy and the directory prefix are bounded by `PosixPathStringCapacity` at their copy sites, and
// each walk hands `getdents64` the whole tail — so the OFFSETS and the bounds are a pair, in different
// functions, with a byte-for-byte overrun of a mapped page as the failure. Checked here rather than
// commented, which is this project's cure ladder: an offset moved without its neighbour fails the build.
export function assertLinuxSearchGeometry()
	let patternBytes = (LinuxSearchPrefixOffset - LinuxSearchPatternOffset) as ParsedInt
	if patternBytes < MaxPathBytes 'patternRegion'
		panic("LinuxRuntime: the search object's pattern region is {patternBytes} bytes and a pattern copy writes up to {MaxPathBytes} (`PosixPathStringCapacity` plus its terminator) — it would run into the directory-prefix scratch above it")
	end 'patternRegion'

	let prefixBytes = (LinuxSearchEntriesOffset - LinuxSearchPrefixOffset) as ParsedInt
	if prefixBytes < MaxPathBytes 'prefixRegion'
		panic("LinuxRuntime: the search object's directory-prefix scratch is {prefixBytes} bytes and `mrt_linux_dir_prefix` writes up to {MaxPathBytes} — it would run into the `getdents64` buffer above it")
	end 'prefixRegion'

	if LinuxSearchEntriesBytes < LinuxSearchMinimumEntriesBytes 'entriesRegion'
		panic("LinuxRuntime: the `getdents64` buffer is {LinuxSearchEntriesBytes} bytes, which cannot hold one worst-case `struct linux_dirent64` ({LinuxSearchMinimumEntriesBytes}) — the kernel would answer EINVAL for every refill")
	end 'entriesRegion'
end 'assertLinuxSearchGeometry'

// A FLOOR for one `struct linux_dirent64` at its longest: the fixed header, a name at the filesystem maximum,
// and one machine word covering the terminator and the 8-byte alignment `d_reclen` is rounded up to. It is
// deliberately a bound rather than the exact rounded size — this is the threshold of a check, not the size of
// an allocation, and a bound that is a few bytes generous cannot be wrong in the direction that matters.
let LinuxDirentMaxNameBytes = 255
let LinuxSearchMinimumEntriesBytes = (LinuxDirentNameOffset as ParsedInt) + LinuxDirentMaxNameBytes + (SpillSlotBytes as ParsedInt)

// ==========================================================================
// THE GREEN-THREAD FLOOR'S LINUX FACTS — arch-neutral, read by both Linux lanes
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐ **A `futex` OPERATION, A CLONE FLAG, A MUTEX STATE AND A WORKER M'S STACK GEOMETRY ARE KERNEL FACTS
// WITH NO ARCHITECTURE IN THEM.** What differs between AArch64 and x86-64 is the SYSCALL NUMBER and the
// register ABI, both of which stay with each backend; every number below is the same on both, and a second
// copy of `LinuxCloneWorkerFlags` or `LinuxLockOffDepth` is two lanes agreeing by hand about a layout the
// kernel decides.

// ⭐ **THE FUTEX OPERATIONS, IN THEIR PRIVATE FORMS.** `FUTEX_WAIT` is 0 and `FUTEX_WAKE` is 1;
// `FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG` is 128 and says the word is never shared across processes, which lets the kernel key
// its hash bucket on the mm rather than on the physical page. Every futex in this compiler is in an
// anonymous private mapping or in the program's own `.data`, so the private form is always the right one —
// and it is measurably cheaper, which is why glibc uses it for exactly these objects.
export let LinuxFutexWaitPrivate = 128
export let LinuxFutexWakePrivate = 129

// ⛔⛔ **THE JOIN's WAIT IS THE *SHARED* `FUTEX_WAIT`, AND THAT IS NOT AN INCONSISTENCY — IT IS THE ONLY
// SPELLING THE KERNEL's OWN WAKE CAN REACH.** A worker M is joined through the `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` word
// (`PosixWaitOffThread`), which the KERNEL zeroes and wakes when the thread ends — and it issues that wake
// with `FLAGS_SHARED`. On an anonymous page a shared key carries `FUT_OFF_MMSHARED` where a private key does
// not, so the two hash to DIFFERENT buckets and a private waiter is never woken.
//
// ⭐ **MEASURED on an aarch64 Ubuntu guest, one C program invoking these syscalls BY NUMBER, one constant
// changed between the two runs:** with
// `FUTEX_WAIT_PRIVATE` the joiner slept through the child's exit and the run was killed at its 20 s bound;
// with this plain `FUTEX_WAIT` it was woken by the FIRST wait and read the cleared word. glibc's
// `pthread_join` passes `LLL_SHARED` for exactly this reason. Every OTHER futex in this file is woken by a
// `FUTEX_WAKE_PRIVATE` this compiler emits, so the private form stays right for all of them.
export let LinuxFutexWaitShared = 0

// `FUTEX_WAKE`'s count: ONE waiter. An auto-reset event releases exactly one waiter by its Win32 contract,
// and the mutex below hands the lock to one successor.
export let LinuxFutexWakeOne = 1

// A NULL `struct timespec *` — `FUTEX_WAIT` with no timeout, i.e. wait until woken.
export let LinuxFutexNoTimeout = 0

// `futex(uaddr, op, val, timeout, uaddr2, val3)`. The last two are meaningless for WAIT and WAKE and MUST
// still be passed: a syscall takes its six argument registers whatever the operation reads, and leaving one
// holding a stale value is how a future requeue-shaped operation would silently pick it up.
export let LinuxFutexUnusedArg = 0

// ⭐⭐ **THE VALUE A `FUTEX_WAIT` EXPECTS, AND WHY IT IS THE UNSIGNALLED STATE RATHER THAN THE SIGNALLED ONE.**
// `FUTEX_WAIT(uaddr, val)` sleeps only while `*uaddr == val`, and returns `-EAGAIN` immediately otherwise.
// So the caller passes the state it is waiting to LEAVE: an event waiter passes `PosixWaitNotSignalled`, and
// a signal that lands between its own test and this call makes the syscall return at once rather than sleep
// through it. That is the whole lost-wakeup argument, and it is the kernel's rather than this file's.
//
// ⚠ The two values themselves are `Targets/Shared/PosixRuntime`'s, because the WORD is: the signal field
// sits at the same offset on every POSIX lane and a shared body (`mrt_host_proc_reap`) publishes into it.

// ⭐ **ONE PAGE, AND IT IS THE SAME PAGE FOR BOTH HOST OBJECTS THIS FILE MAPS** — a wait object and an OS
// thread's slot block. Named once because `mmap`'s granularity is what decides it, not either object's
// contents: a wait object uses two words of it and a slot block as many as the scheduler asks for.
export let LinuxHostObjectPageBytes = 4096

// ⭐⭐ **THE RECURSIVE FUTEX MUTEX `osLockInit` BUILDS INTO THE CALLER'S REGION.**
//
// ⛔ **RECURSIVE IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT WAS MEASURED AS A HANG ON THE OTHER LANE.** A Win32
// `CRITICAL_SECTION` is re-entrant by its owning thread and `SchedRuntime` relies on it in so many words
// (*"a nested `LeaveCriticalSection` releases nothing"*); the first arm64-macOS `async` build hung in
// `__slab_refill` on a NORMAL pthread mutex taken twice down one call chain
// (`Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinLockInitChunk` records it). So the same op must mean the same thing
// here, and a raw futex is not re-entrant — the owner and the depth beside it are what make it so.
//
// The three words: a futex at offset 0 (0 = free, 1 = held with no waiter, 2 = held and someone is asleep on
// it), the owning thread's `gettid`, and how many times that owner has taken it.
//
// ⚠ **THE OWNER WORD IS READ WITHOUT SYNCHRONISATION AND THAT IS SOUND**, for a reason that does not
// generalise: the only value a thread can act on is ITS OWN tid, and no other thread can write that there —
// a non-owner reads 0 or somebody else's tid, and both send it to the acquire path.
//
// ⚠ **THE REGION IS NOT ZEROED BY ANYONE ELSE, SO `osLockInit` WRITES ALL THREE WORDS.** Its allocator is the
// slab, whose pages come from `mmap` and ARE zero on first use — but a region reused after a free is not, and
// relying on the difference is exactly the kind of "works today" this compiler's assertions exist to refuse.
//
// ⚠ **A NESTED LEAVE DECREMENTS THE DEPTH AND RELEASES NOTHING**, which is the property `SchedRuntime`'s
// header names in so many words: only the outermost leave touches the futex word.
export let LinuxLockOffFutex = 0x00 as MachineWord
export let LinuxLockOffOwner = 0x08 as MachineWord
export let LinuxLockOffDepth = 0x10 as MachineWord

// The three states of the futex word above, named because a bare 2 at a `cas` site says nothing about which
// of this file's several 2s it is.
//
// ⭐⭐ **REACHING CONTENDED IS A THREE-COMPARE-AND-SWAP PROTOCOL, AND EVERY LANE's `lock_enter` RUNS IT.** A
// waiter must mark the word CONTENDED before it sleeps, or the releaser takes the fast path and never wakes
// it:
//   1. `0 -> 2` — the lock came free while we were looking; take it, conservatively marked, because we
//      cannot know whether a third thread is already asleep on it.
//   2. `1 -> 2` — it is held with no waiter recorded; record one. The verdict is ignored: a failure means it
//      is already 2 (fine) or that it just became free (the next trip's step 1 takes it).
//   3. `FUTEX_WAIT(word, 2)` — sleep only while it is still 2, which the KERNEL checks, so the window
//      between step 2 and here cannot lose a wakeup.
export let LinuxMutexFree = 0
export let LinuxMutexHeld = 1
export let LinuxMutexHeldContended = 2

// The depth an outermost acquire records, and the value `atomicRmw`'s DISPLACED word carries when the
// release was the last one and nobody was asleep.
export let LinuxMutexFirstEntry = 1

// `atomicRmw(add)` with this operand is the release decrement: it answers the word it DISPLACED, which is
// what says whether a waiter has to be woken (`LinuxMutexHeldContended`) or not (`LinuxMutexHeld`). Reading
// the displaced value is what makes those two cases distinguishable without a second atomic.
//
// ⚠ **THE OWNER AND DEPTH ARE CLEARED BEFORE THIS DECREMENT, NEVER AFTER.** The instant the futex word goes
// free another thread may take it and write its own tid there; clearing afterwards would erase the new
// owner's claim and let a third thread believe it holds a lock it does not.
export let LinuxMutexReleaseStep = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

// ⭐⭐ **THE PER-OS-THREAD SLOT BLOCK, WHOSE BASE IS THE ARCH's OWN THREAD POINTER** — `TPIDR_EL0` on AArch64,
// the FS base on x86-64. A static image links no libc, so there is no `pthread_getspecific` to call and no
// TSD array to index — and by the same token NOTHING ELSE READS OR WRITES THE WRITABLE THREAD POINTER, which
// makes it free for the scheduler to define. One page per OS thread, `mmap`'d by `mrt_host_tls_alloc` the
// first time that thread asks for a slot.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO ARCHES READ IT AT VERY DIFFERENT PRICE.** `TPIDR_EL0` is an ordinary register; the FS base
// needs `arch_prctl(ARCH_GET_FS)`, a SYSCALL — which is why the x64 lane answers *"does this thread have a
// block?"* from a process-wide `.data` flag instead (`X64LinuxRuntime.x64LinuxEmitStoreErrno` carries why
// that is sound and not an approximation).
//
// ⚠ **KEY 0 IS NEVER HANDED OUT, EXACTLY AS ON DARWIN, AND FOR THE SAME REASON.** The Std tier publishes
// `key * 8 + tlsSlotArrayBase(target)` and every reader treats a ZERO offset as *"`__sched_init_procs` has
// not run"*; the base is 0 here, so a key of 0 would make that offset 0 and every current-P read in the
// program would answer NULL for ever — a scheduler that silently never runs. The counter below therefore
// pre-increments, and the chunk asserts the result rather than trusting the arithmetic.

// How many keys one block holds — DERIVED from the page and the slot width, and a FUNCTION rather than a
// `let` for `runtimeFrameSlots`' language reason: a global initializer folds `/` as a float.
export function linuxTlsSlotCount() returns ParsedInt
	return LinuxHostObjectPageBytes / SpillSlotBytes
end 'linuxTlsSlotCount'

// The first key handed out, and the reason the counter pre-increments — see the block above.
//
// ⭐ **SLOT 0 IS NOT MERELY UNISSUED, IT IS THIS THREAD's `errno`** — `arm64LinuxEmitErrnoAddr` on one lane,
// `x64LinuxEmitStoreErrno`/`x64LinuxEmitLoadErrno` on the other. The two
// reasons stack rather than compete: a key of 0 would publish a slot offset of 0, which every current-P
// reader treats as *"the scheduler has not started"*, and the word that offset addresses is already spoken
// for.
export let LinuxTlsFirstKey = 1

// ⭐ **THE THREE VERBS ONLY THIS LANE SPELLS AS A CHUNK, SO THEY KEEP THE LANE'S INFIX.**
// `Targets/Shared/PosixRuntime`'s header states the rule: a name goes under the shared `mrt_host_` prefix
// when the VERB is a shared host-surface verb served by every lane. These three are not — on macOS `osLockEnter` and
// `osLockLeave` are a bare `pthread_mutex_lock`/`_unlock` import with no chunk at all, and `tlsSetValue` is
// one `pthread_setspecific`. Only a raw static image needs a body, and a body one lane builds wears that
// lane's name, exactly as `mrt_linux_glob_match` does.
export let LinuxLockEnterName = b"mrt_linux_lock_enter"
export let LinuxLockLeaveName = b"mrt_linux_lock_leave"
export let LinuxTlsSetName = b"mrt_linux_tls_set"

// The per-OS-thread slot counter `mrt_host_tls_alloc` hands out of. (The environment vector's word is
// `LinuxRuntime.LinuxEnvpLabel` — both lanes capture it, so it is not this one's to declare.)
export let LinuxTlsNextKeyLabel = b"__mrt_tls_next_key"

// ⭐ **THE GREEN-THREAD FLOOR'S `.data` WORD, ON THE FLOOR'S OWN BIT AND ON BOTH LINUX LANES.** Split from
// the errno word rather than folded into it because the two conditions are genuinely different: a program
// that reads a file carries the errno word and no scheduler, and a pure-`async` program carries this one and
// no errno word.
//
// ⚠ **NO CPU TEST**: the counter, the page it indexes and the pre-increment that keeps key 0 unissued are the
// same fact on both Linux lanes, and only the instruction that reads the thread pointer differs.
export function linuxGreenThreadGlobals(usage RuntimeUsage, target Target) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	if target.os != Os.linux or not usage.usesGt 'notThisLane'
		return out
	end 'notThisLane'

	// The per-OS-thread slot counter. `__sched_init_procs` is its only caller and runs during bring-up, which
	// is what makes the plain read-modify-write in `mrt_host_tls_alloc` sound.
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: LinuxTlsNextKeyLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	return out
end 'linuxGreenThreadGlobals'

// `clock_nanosleep`'s `flags`: 0 is a RELATIVE request, `TIMER_ABSTIME` (1) an absolute deadline. The op
// carries a duration, so this is the relative one — and naming it is what stops the 0 reading as "no flags I
// happened to need".
export let LinuxSleepIsRelative = 0

// Its `remain` out-param. NULL discards the unslept remainder, which this chunk has no channel to report and
// whose one consumer re-checks its own deadline anyway (see the header).
export let LinuxSleepDiscardsRemainder = 0

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE WORKER M's `clone` FLAGS — `pthread_create`'s set, and every bit has a reason.** The child is
// a THREAD of this process, not a process: it must see the same heap the scheduler put the green threads in,
// the same descriptor table the IO ran on, and the same `getpid()` every other part of the runtime reads.
//
//   • `CLONE_VM` — one address space. Without it the M would run the scheduler over a copy-on-write snapshot
//     and publish its work where nobody could see it. It is what makes this a thread at all.
//   • `CLONE_FS` — one filesystem context, so `Directory.setCurrent` on any M is the process's cwd, which is
//     the semantics every other lane's threads have.
//   • `CLONE_FILES` — one descriptor table: a file a green thread opened on one M is readable on the M that
//     resumes it after a park.
//   • `CLONE_SIGHAND` — one signal-handler table. The kernel REQUIRES it with `CLONE_THREAD` (and requires
//     `CLONE_VM` with it), so it is a precondition rather than a preference.
//   • `CLONE_THREAD` — one thread group. `getpid()` then answers the same value on every M (the pipe-name
//     uniquifier and `mrt_host_proc_*` both read it), and the entry stub's `exit_group` at the end of `main`
//     ends the whole program rather than leaving parked Ms behind.
//   • `CLONE_SETTLS` — the new M's thread pointer (`TPIDR_EL0` on AArch64, the FS base on x86-64) = the slot
//     block this chunk maps for it. On both lanes the thread pointer IS the per-OS-thread slot base
//     (`mrt_host_tls_alloc`), and `__sched_worker_loop`'s very first
//     act is `tlsSetValue(index, p)` through it — so an M cloned without this would panic in
//     `mrt_linux_tls_set` before it adopted its P.
//   • `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` — the kernel zeroes `PosixWaitOffThread` and wakes it when the thread ends,
//     which IS the join `__sched_stop_workers` performs. See `LinuxFutexWaitShared` for the wake's flavour.
//
// ⚠ **`CLONE_SYSVSEM` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT** though glibc passes it: nothing in this runtime touches a
// System V semaphore, and a flag with no consumer is a claim nobody can check.
//
// ⛔ **THE EXIT SIGNAL IS ZERO — the low byte carries none.** `CLONE_THREAD` forbids one, and a `SIGCHLD` here
// would be the L5 child-process spelling pasted onto a thread.
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED, not transcribed** — a C program on the aarch64 guest issuing `svc` with x8 = 220 directly:
// this exact word, in this exact argument order `(flags, stack, ptid, tls, ctid)`, started a thread that
// read the `tls` page back out of `TPIDR_EL0` (and a slot the parent had written in it), saw the same
// `getpid()` as its parent, took its argument off the stack top, and ended with `exit` leaving the process
// alive — which `exit_group` would not have.
let LinuxCloneVm = 0x00000100
let LinuxCloneFs = 0x00000200
let LinuxCloneFiles = 0x00000400
let LinuxCloneSighand = 0x00000800
let LinuxCloneThread = 0x00010000
let LinuxCloneSetTls = 0x00080000
let LinuxCloneChildClearTid = 0x00200000
export let LinuxCloneWorkerFlags = LinuxCloneVm or LinuxCloneFs or LinuxCloneFiles or LinuxCloneSighand or LinuxCloneThread or LinuxCloneSetTls or LinuxCloneChildClearTid

// ⭐⭐ **A WORKER M's OWN STACK: ONE MEGABYTE, WITH A PROT_NONE PAGE UNDER IT — AT THE BOTTOM OF THE REGION**,
// because stacks grow down and the page the deepest frame reaches first is the lowest one. The size is `CreateThread`'s
// default reserve on the lane this op was shaped for — `osThreadCreate` is `CreateThread(NULL, 0, …)` on
// Windows and 0 means "the image's default", which is 1 MiB — so the three lanes give an M the same order of
// stack rather than each inventing one. It is generous by a factor of about a thousand for what actually
// runs here: an M's stack carries `__sched_worker_loop` and the runtime chunks it calls, and NOT a green
// thread's frames (a switch moves SP to the GT's own stack) nor the syscall shim's (that is the P's own
// `POffSystemStackSP`, a separate 64 KB region). `MAP_ANONYMOUS` commits lazily, so the unused margin costs
// address space and no memory.
//
// ⛔ **THE GUARD PAGE IS NOT DEFENSIVE.** Without it an overflow writes into whatever the kernel mapped
// below — silently, with the corruption surfacing somewhere else entirely. `mprotect(PROT_NONE)` turns that
// into a SIGSEGV at the instruction that did it. MEASURED on the guest: a touch of the guarded page ends the
// toucher with signal 11.
let LinuxWorkerStackBytes = 0x100000
export let LinuxWorkerStackGuardBytes = LinuxHostObjectPageBytes
export let LinuxWorkerRegionBytes = LinuxWorkerStackGuardBytes + LinuxWorkerStackBytes

// ⭐ **THE TWO WORDS THE CHILD READS OFF ITS OWN STACK TOP: the entry point and its argument.** `clone`'s
// child resumes with x0 = 0 and NOTHING ELSE it can rely on — no return address, and a frame pointer still
// aimed at the parent's stack — so the pair cannot be handed over in registers or in this chunk's frame
// slots, both of which belong to the parent. musl's `aarch64/clone.s` solves it the same way, and this is
// that solution: the parent writes them below the stack top, the child loads them through its own SP. 16
// bytes keeps SP 16-aligned, which AAPCS64 requires at the `blr`.
export let LinuxWorkerStackSeedBytes = 16

// ⭐ **THE ONE STATE OF THE JOIN WORD AT `PosixWaitOffThread` THAT THIS COMPILER GETS TO CHOOSE.**
// `mrt_host_thread_create` writes it before the `clone` and `mrt_host_wait_one`'s join arm passes it to
// `FUTEX_WAIT` as the value it is waiting to LEAVE. ⚠ **THE EXITED STATE IS NOT A CONSTANT HERE BECAUSE IT IS
// NOT THIS COMPILER'S TO NAME**: `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` writes a 32-bit ZERO, always, so the join's exit test
// is each arch's own zero test on the word — the instruction that already means "is zero" — rather than a
// compare against a number we would only be re-deriving from the kernel's behaviour.
export let LinuxThreadRunning = 1

// What a worker M's own `exit` reports. Nothing reads it — a thread's status is not waitable and the join
// answers `WAIT_OBJECT_0` for every M — and 0 is what a thread that ran to the end of its loop means.
export let LinuxWorkerExitStatus = 0

export let LinuxWaitMapFailedMessage = b"panic: the green-thread scheduler could not map a wait object (mmap failed)\n"
export let LinuxWaitKindMessage = b"panic: a wait object of a kind this backend does not mint reached mrt_host_wait_new or mrt_host_wait_one -- a Linux lane mints wake events, child processes and worker-M threads, and nothing else is waitable\n"

// ⛔ **A FAILED GUARD IS A PANIC, NOT A SILENT UNGUARDED STACK.** `mprotect` on a page the chunk itself just
// mapped can only fail if the kernel refuses `PROT_NONE` on an anonymous private mapping; carrying on would
// hand the scheduler an M whose overflow corrupts a neighbour instead of faulting.
export let LinuxWorkerGuardFailedMessage = b"panic: the green-thread scheduler could not protect a worker M's stack guard page (mprotect failed)\n"

// Where the two seed words sit relative to the child's entry SP — the entry point first, its argument
// second. Named because the child reads them from a DIFFERENT function than the parent writes them in, and a
// bare 0 and 8 at two sites is the one fact this pair must agree on.
export let LinuxWorkerSeedEntryOffset = 0 as MachineWord
export let LinuxWorkerSeedArgOffset = 8 as MachineWord

// `CreateThread`'s NULL — what `SchedRuntime.buildSchedWakeOrSpawn`'s `spawnIt` tests to decide it must
// release the P it claimed. It is the handle value, not an errno: this op reports failure by handing back no
// handle at all, exactly as the Win32 call it stands for does.
export let LinuxNoThreadStarted = 0

export let LinuxWaitOnDescriptorMessage = b"panic: WaitForSingleObject was reached with a DESCRIPTOR or a null handle, where only a wake event, a worker M or a child process is waitable\n"

// ⭐ **THE LOCK REGION THE Std TIER ALLOCATES MUST HOLD THIS LANE'S MUTEX.** `osLockInit`'s caller
// slab-allocates `OsLockBytes` and hands the pointer over; the three words below are written into whatever
// it is given. The Std constant is sized for the widest lock across the targets this compiler serves and
// says so, but a COMMENT cannot keep that true — so this is the check, at chunk-build time, on the lane that
// would be corrupted.
export function assertLinuxLockFits()
	let needed = (LinuxLockOffDepth as ParsedInt) + SpillSlotBytes
	if OsLockBytes < needed 'tooSmall'
		panic("LinuxRuntime: `OsLockBytes` is {OsLockBytes} but this lane's recursive futex mutex needs {needed} bytes — `osLockInit` would write the owner and depth words past the region the allocator gave it and corrupt whatever it placed after, with no diagnostic anywhere")
	end 'tooSmall'
end 'assertLinuxLockFits'

export let LinuxTlsMapFailedMessage = b"panic: the green-thread scheduler could not map this OS thread's slot block (mmap failed)\n"
export let LinuxTlsExhaustedMessage = b"panic: the green-thread scheduler asked for more per-OS-thread slots than one page holds\n"

// A key scales to a byte offset by the slot width — `key * 8`, spelled as a shift because that is the same
// `index * 8` the Std tier publishes (`SchedRuntime.tlsSlotArrayBase`) and the two must agree exactly.
export let LinuxTlsKeyScaleBits = 3 as ShiftCount

// ⭐⭐ **`clone`'s FIVE ARGUMENTS, AND FOUR OF THEM ARE THE ABSENCE OF A THREAD.** The flag word's low byte is
// the signal the child sends its parent when it dies, and `SIGCHLD` alone — with none of `CLONE_VM`,
// `CLONE_FS`, `CLONE_FILES`, `CLONE_SIGHAND` or `CLONE_THREAD` — is precisely `fork`. A NULL stack means the
// child keeps the parent's SP over a copy-on-write mapping, which is what makes returning from the syscall in
// two processes sound.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE COPY-ON-WRITE ADDRESS SPACE IS WHAT MAKES THE CHILD's PATH TO `execve` SAFE.** Everything it does
// between the clone and the exec — three `dup3`s, a `chdir`, a write down the status pipe — happens where the
// parent's heap cannot be touched, and its frame-slot reads address its OWN copy of the parent's frame while
// the parent is blocked on the status read until the exec settles it.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE TWO ARCHES ORDER `clone`'s LAST TWO ARGUMENTS DIFFERENTLY — AArch64 `(tls, ctid)`, x86-64
// `(ctid, tls)` — WHICH IS WHY THE ARGUMENT SHUFFLE STAYS WITH EACH BACKEND AND ONLY THESE VALUES ARE SHARED.**
// All five are NULL for a child process, so the disagreement cannot bite here; it bites in
// `mrt_host_thread_create`, where both are live, and it fails as a HANG rather than an error.
export let LinuxCloneExitSignalChild = 17
export let LinuxCloneParentStack = 0
export let LinuxCloneNoParentTidOut = 0
export let LinuxCloneNoThreadPointer = 0
export let LinuxCloneNoChildTidOut = 0

// `wait4`'s options word. The reap of a child whose exec failed is the one place this file waits without
// `WNOHANG`, and it cannot hang — the child has already written the pipe and called `exit`. The `rusage`
// argument beside it is `Targets/Shared/PosixRuntime`'s `PosixReapDiscardsRusage`.
export let LinuxReapUntilItExits = 0

// `dup3`'s flag word. 0 leaves the new descriptor WITHOUT close-on-exec, which is the point: these three are
// the only descriptors that must survive the exec.
//
// ⛔⛔ **`dup3` REFUSES `oldfd == newfd` WHERE `dup2` RETURNS IT UNCHANGED (`EINVAL`), SO EVERY LANE's CHILD
// GUARDS THE CALL AND THE GUARD IS NOT DEFENSIVE.** That case is not exotic: a stream whose stdio kind is
// `inherit` reaches the startup record as the parent's own 0, 1 or 2, so a plain `Subprocess.run` with no
// redirection hits it three times.
//
// ⚠ **THE OLD DESCRIPTOR MUST BE UNTAGGED FIRST**: a pipe end reaches the record as a `PosixHandleKindShift`
// handle, while an inherited standard stream and a redirected file reach it as bare descriptors.
export let LinuxDup3NoFlags = 0

// `O_CLOEXEC`. Every pipe this lane opens carries it, because there is no `POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT` here
// to invert the inheritance default for the whole exec — see `Arm64PosixRuntime.buildArm64PosixPipeChunk`.
export let LinuxOpenCloseOnExec = 0o2000000

// What a child that could not `execve` exits with — the shell's own "command found but could not be run",
// and what `execvp` failing inside `system()` reports. The parent does not read it: the status pipe carries
// the real reason, and this is what a program that never asked would see.
export let LinuxExecFailedExitCode = 127

// ==========================================================================
// The SOCKET band's Linux facts — arch-neutral, and both lanes read them
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`MSG_NOSIGNAL` ON LINUX. ⚠ IT IS 0x4000 HERE AND 0x80000 ON DARWIN, AND MISTAKING ONE FOR THE
// OTHER KILLS THE PROCESS.** A `sendto` on a stream socket whose peer has gone raises SIGPIPE, whose
// default disposition is TERMINATE, and this backend installs no signal handler for it — so without the
// flag the program dies by signal instead of reporting `__ManagedSocketError.sendFailed`. MEASURED on an
// aarch64 Linux guest with a `socketpair` whose read end was closed: with 0x4000 the raw `sendto` answered
// `-EPIPE` and the probe exited 0; with a flag word of 0 the identical probe died with exit 141.
// `StdToArm64Conversion.DarwinMsgNoSignal` carries the other value and the same measurement.
export let LinuxMsgNoSignal = 0x4000

// `sendto`/`recvfrom`'s peer address and its length. A CONNECTED stream socket has neither to give, and a
// NULL pair is what makes the six-argument call mean the four-argument `send`/`recv` AArch64 and x86-64
// have no syscall for.
export let LinuxSocketNoPeerAddress = 0

// ⭐ **HOW FAR TO SHIFT AN ANSWER TO REPLICATE ITS SIGN** — one less than a machine word's width, spent by
// the `answer | (answer asr 63)` fold each isel's socket band applies to a raw syscall result. A syscall
// reports failure as `-errno` where the Std tier's socket ops promise exactly -1, and that fold is the
// cheapest total translation between the two (see each isel's `emitSyscallFailureSentinel`).
export let LinuxSyscallSignShift = (MachineWordBits - 1) as ShiftCount
